{"ts": 1789717353.2387872, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "I submitted short feature 33, short feature 38 and long feature 19, with a predicted out-of-sample Sharpe of 0.12 and a standard deviation of 0.30. The combination scored 0.959 in-sample, which does not clear the search-adjusted bar of 1.020.\n\n- **Single features look like noise.** Over 5,000 days the standard error of an annualized Sharpe is about 0.22, and the 40 single-feature Sharpes had a root-mean-square of 0.24. A chi-square test gives a p-value of about 0.23. The largest single Sharpe, 0.62 in absolute value, is about what 40 noise draws would produce.\n- **Why these three.** I took the three largest single-feature Sharpes. The combined score of 0.959 is almost exactly the sum of the three divided by \u221a3, so the features look independent. With independent features, those top three are the best combination of three, and further searching would only add selection bias. I did not search further to clear the bar.\n- **How I set the prediction.** The data supports a true Sharpe spread across features of only about 0.09. Shrinking the in-sample estimates accordingly keeps about 14% of the 0.959, which gives roughly 0.12. The 0.30 standard deviation combines about 0.1 of uncertainty in the true Sharpe with sampling noise from an out-of-sample window whose length I don't know.\n\nThe Gmail and Google Calendar connectors are not authorized and can't be used until you authorize them in your claude.ai connector settings. They weren't needed for this task."}
